Mild transient lower back stiffness following unaccustomed light lifting without radiculopathy
Minor Ailments and Upsets
Lumbar paraspinal stiffness, Pain with spinal movement, Normal lower extremity neurology

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MAU-027
Reason for Visit: Acute onset of severe lower back pain and immobility requiring neurological assessment to rule out cauda equina syndrome or acute disc herniation.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted sudden severe lumbar pain following lifting. Urgent physical assessment was necessary prior to the next in-hours period to test lower extremity neurology, assess bladder/bowel function, and definitively rule out cauda equina syndrome or acute spinal cord compression which would require immediate surgical intervention.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I lifted a box and my lower back completely seized up; I can barely stand up straight.
History of Present Illness: Patient describes bilateral dull aching stiffness localized to the lumbar paraspinal muscles, starting abruptly after unaccustomed light lifting earlier today. Reports pain exacerbation with dynamic lumbar flexion or extension, and relief of structural discomfort with rest and supine positioning. Complete absence of radiating leg pain, distal paresthesias, or saddle anesthesia. Intact bowel and bladder control confirmed.
Functional Status: Antalgic gait, transitioning from sit to stand is slow and painful.
Social/Safety Concerns: Impaired mobility temporarily limits ability to perform heavier household tasks.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 130/80 | HR: 78 | Temp: 36.6 C | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 80 Kg
General: Alert, well-appearing, moving carefully to avoid pain exacerbation.
Physical Exam: Lumbar spine examination shows bilateral lumbar paraspinal muscle spasm and stiffness. Pain is elicited with spinal movement (flexion/extension). Neurological examination reveals normal lower extremity global muscle strength (5/5). Intact deep tendon reflexes in the patella and Achilles bilaterally. Preserved dermatomal sensation across the lower limbs. Negative straight leg raise test bilaterally. Normal intact perianal sensation reported.
Environmental Observations: Heavy boxes present in the living area.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Patient taking no regular analgesics.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Mild transient lower back stiffness following unaccustomed light lifting without radiculopathy - Status: Stable
Differentials:

Cauda Equina Syndrome - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Intact bowel and bladder control, normal lower limb neurology, and absence of saddle anesthesia.

Lumbar Disc Herniation with Radiculopathy - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Complete absence of radiating leg pain, negative straight leg raise, and normal sensory/motor exam.

Vertebral Compression Fracture - Ruled out/unlikely due to: No history of significant trauma, absence of focal midline spinal tenderness, and low-risk demographic.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient suffered an acute, severely painful lumbar event. Given the mechanism of lifting and the profound immobility, an urgent after-hours neurological examination was mandated to safely exclude critical red-flag conditions like cauda equina syndrome or an acute epidural compromise. By confirming perfectly intact lower extremity neurology and attributing the immobility to paraspinal muscle spasm, the assessment safely averted an unnecessary ED transfer and emergency MRI, allowing for conservative home management.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescription for a short course of scheduled NSAIDs (ibuprofen) combined with paracetamol. Recommendation to maintain gentle mobility and avoid strict bed rest.
Positive Behavioral Change: Set a timer to stand up and perform gentle pelvic tilts for two minutes every hour while awake, linking pain relief directly to maintaining mobility and preventing muscle shortening.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated.
DME/Safety (Optional): Heat pack for paraspinal muscle relaxation.
Follow-up: Routine GP review if the pain does not improve within 1 week or if new neurological symptoms develop.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to Emergency Department or call 000 if you suddenly lose control of your bladder or bowels, experience numbness around your groin or buttocks, or develop sudden weakness in your legs.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is clinical muscular strain; symptoms generally improve rapidly but can take weeks to fully resolve.
Clinical Course: The acute severe spasm should decrease significantly over the next 48 to 72 hours, transitioning to a dull stiffness.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate medical attention if pain begins shooting down past your knee, or if you notice foot drop or leg weakness.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The muscles in your lower back went into protective spasm after being overstretched. Strict bedrest makes this worse. Regular pain relief allows you to move, and movement signals the muscles to relax.
Actionable Steps: Take ibuprofen with food. Do not stay in bed for more than a few hours at a time. Use a heat pack on the lower back for 20 minutes every few hours.

SMS INFORMATION

